AIM
To write a C program to find the common elements between two arrays and analyze its time and space complexity
ALGORITHM
1. Start.
2. Initialize two arrays.
3. Traverse the first array element by element.
4. Compare each element with all elements of the second array.
5. If a matching element is found, print it.
6. Continue until all elements of the first array are checked.
7. Stop
PSEUDOCODE
Algorithm CommonElements(A, B)

    for i = 0 to length(A) - 1 do
        for j = 0 to length(B) - 1 do
            if A[i] = B[j] then
                print A[i]
                break
            end if
        end for
    end for

End Algorithm
SOURCE CODE (C)

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int A[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int B[] = {3, 4, 5, 6, 7};
    int n = 5, m = 5;
    int i, j;

    printf("Common elements: ");

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            if (A[i] == B[j]) {
                printf("%d ", A[i]);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
INPUT
Array A = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Array B = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7]


 OUTPUT
Common elements: 3 4 5

COMPLEXITY ANALYSIS
Let \(n\) be the number of elements in array A and \(m\) be the number of elements in array B.
- Time Complexity: \(O(n \times m)\) — each element of A may be compared with every element of B.
- Auxiliary Space Complexity: \(O(1)\) — only a fixed number of variables are used.

RESULT
Thus, the C program successfully identifies the common elements between two arrays. The worst-case time complexity is \(O(nm)\), and the auxiliary space complexity is \(O(1)\).
